{"test_id": "B01-001", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "A healthcare provider's patient monitoring systems and MRI machines have been designated as CII. Their hospital administration system (patient records, billing, appointments) shares the same enterprise network. Does CCoP 2.0 mandatory compliance extend to the hospital administration system?", "scenario_sector": "healthcare", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "not-applicable", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 mandatory compliance applies to the designated CII and the cyber operating environment defined for that CII, not automatically to every system on the same enterprise network. The hospital administration system does not become in-scope solely because it shares network infrastructure with the patient-monitoring and MRI CII. However, if it forms part of the cyber operating environment of the designated CII or creates a path that can affect the CII, the interface and related dependencies must be assessed and secured. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.2.1, §1.4.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §7.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 mandatory compliance applies only to systems within the digital boundary of the designated CII, not all systems on the enterprise network", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 1.2, Cybersecurity Act Section 11", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The digital boundary is jointly determined by CSA, the CIIO, and the Sector Lead — it is not the enterprise network perimeter", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 1.2, RESPONSE-TO-FEEDBACK Q2.2-2.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CSA recommends extending CCoP cybersecurity capabilities organization-wide even beyond mandatory scope", "source": "RESPONSE-TO-FEEDBACK Q2.2-2.3", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Systems outside the digital boundary are not subject to mandatory CCoP 2.0 cybersecurity audits", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 1.2", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP applies to the entire organization once any system is designated", "All systems sharing the same network as CII are automatically in scope"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific clause mandates organization-wide compliance", "Inventing exceptions for healthcare sector not stated in CCoP"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "1.4.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "digital_boundary_determination", "related_sections": ["CCoP Annex A"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["section 7", "2.2"]}}
{"test_id": "B01-002", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "We are a water utility whose SCADA system controlling treatment plant operations has been designated as CII under CCoP 1.0. CCoP 2.0 came into effect in July 2022 and contains approximately 220 clauses — more than double the CCoP 1.0 requirements. Are we required to comply with all new CCoP 2.0 clauses from the effective date, or is there a transition grace period?", "scenario_sector": "water", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 applies to all CIIOs from its effective date (July 2022). However, CSA provided a transition arrangement: CIIOs were expected to develop a gap assessment and remediation roadmap rather than achieve full compliance overnight. For recurring requirements (such as annual risk assessments or biennial VA/PT), CSA clarified that deadlines are based on when the previous instance was performed under CCoP 1.0, so existing schedules carry forward. The jump from approximately 102 to approximately 220 clauses created significant implementation pressure, particularly for smaller CIIOs in sectors like water. CIIOs that could not meet specific new requirements within a reasonable period were expected to submit waiver requests under Section 11(7) of the Cybersecurity Act, with documented compensating controls and remediation timelines. Ongoing CSA sector engagement supported CIIOs through the transition.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 became effective July 2022 and supersedes CCoP 1.0 — all designated CIIOs must comply", "source": "CCoP 2.0, Cybersecurity Act Section 11", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CSA clarified that recurring requirement deadlines (e.g., annual risk assessment, biennial VA/PT) carry over from CCoP 1.0 schedules — gaps are not reset to zero", "source": "RESPONSE-TO-FEEDBACK Q6.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CIIOs unable to comply with specific new clauses should submit waiver requests under Section 11(7) with compensating controls and remediation timeline", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7)", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 expanded from approximately 102 to approximately 220 clauses — a 116% increase that created transition pressure for smaller CIIOs", "source": "CCoP 2.0 implementation background", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 does not apply to existing CIIOs until re-designation", "CCoP 1.0 compliance is sufficient for legacy CII systems"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing a specific grace period number of months not stated in CCoP", "Claiming CSA issued formal deferral for specific sectors"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Applicability", "clause_reference": ["1.4.3", "1.4.4", "1.5.1", "1.5.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "ccop_version_transition", "related_sections": ["RESPONSE-TO-FEEDBACK Q6.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-003", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "A government agency operates IT systems that are designated as CII. They also comply with IM8 (Instruction Manual for ICT and Smart Systems Management), which is the government-wide ICT security framework. Our CISO says we only need one audit — either IM8 or CCoP — and that they cover the same requirements. Is this correct?", "scenario_sector": "government", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "partial", "expected_response": "(no content change required) — but the IM8 reference should be retained in prose only, not as a 'Clause Ref'. The substantive claim that CCoP and IM8 are distinct frameworks is supported by CCoP 2.0 §1.4.5 ('The CIIO's obligations under this Code are in addition to its other obligations under the Act and other laws…').", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 is mandated by the Cybersecurity Act enforced by CSA; IM8 is a separate GovTech framework — they are distinct regulatory obligations", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11, GovTech IM8", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CSA acknowledged the IM8/CCoP dual compliance burden and committed to harmonization including possible mutual audit recognition", "source": "RESPONSE-TO-FEEDBACK on regulatory overlap", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "As of CCoP 2.0, CIIOs must confirm with both CSA and GovTech whether mutual recognition applies before assuming a single audit satisfies both", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "IM8 applies to all government ICT systems while CCoP 2.0 applies only to designated CII systems within the digital boundary", "source": "GovTech IM8 framework scope", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["IM8 compliance fully satisfies CCoP 2.0 requirements", "A single audit automatically satisfies both IM8 and CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific clause exempts government sector CIIOs from CCoP", "Inventing a formal MOU between GovTech and CSA on audit equivalence"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Applicability", "clause_reference": ["1.4.5"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "multi_regulator_applicability", "related_sections": ["Section 2 audit requirements"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-004", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our bank's core banking system is designated as CII. We are migrating it to a cloud infrastructure operated by a major cloud service provider (AWS Singapore region). Does CCoP 2.0 still apply to our core banking system after the cloud migration, or does the cloud provider take on CCoP compliance responsibility?", "scenario_sector": "banking", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 continues to apply to your core banking system after cloud migration. CSA has clarified that CCoP requirements apply regardless of the hosting model — cloud migration does not transfer or reduce the CIIO's compliance obligations. The cloud provider operates the underlying infrastructure, but your organization as the CIIO retains full responsibility for CCoP 2.0 compliance of the CII system. Shared responsibility models apply: the cloud provider handles physical security and infrastructure controls, but access control, monitoring, incident response, and other CCoP requirements remain your responsibility. You must ensure your cloud deployment configuration satisfies CCoP requirements, and the digital boundary must be updated with CSA to reflect the new hosting architecture. Cloud-hosted CII must also be assessed for data sovereignty implications and whether CSA's requirements on logging and audit access are met in the cloud environment.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements apply regardless of hosting model — cloud migration does not transfer compliance obligations to the cloud provider", "source": "CSA guidance on cloud-hosted CII, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The CIIO retains full CCoP 2.0 accountability even when infrastructure is outsourced to a cloud service provider", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11, CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The digital boundary must be updated with CSA to reflect cloud-hosted architecture changes", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope section", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Shared responsibility models apply: cloud provider handles infrastructure, CIIO handles access control, monitoring, incident response per CCoP", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Cloud migration transfers CCoP compliance responsibility to the cloud provider", "CCoP 2.0 does not apply to cloud-hosted systems"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming AWS or other CSPs are CSA-certified and this satisfies CCoP", "Inventing specific cloud exemptions in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Scope", "clause_reference": ["5.1.3", "8.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "cloud_scope_determination", "related_sections": ["CCoP Section 5 Protection"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-005", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our energy company has been informed by CSA that we are being considered for CII designation. We have 30 days to submit our views. Our CEO wants to appeal the potential designation because it will impose significant compliance costs. What recourse does the organization have, and what happens during the review period?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "Under Section 17 of the Cybersecurity Act, an owner aggrieved by the Commissioner's decision to designate their system as CII may appeal to the Minister within 30 days of the designation notice. The appeal must state the circumstances, issues, and grounds, supported by facts, evidence, and arguments. The Minister may establish an Appeals Advisory Panel for technically complex matters. Critically, unless the Minister specifically allows otherwise, the designation remains in effect during the appeal — your organization must continue complying with CCoP 2.0 requirements while the appeal is processed. The Minister's decision is final and can confirm, vary, or reverse the designation. Before formal designation is issued, CSA typically engages the potential CIIO in discussion. Your organization should prepare a substantive submission explaining why the system does not meet the Section 7 criteria (necessity for continuous essential service delivery with debilitating effect if compromised, located in Singapore).", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Under Section 17, appeal to the Minister must be filed within 30 days of the designation notice", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 17", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The designation remains in effect during the appeal unless the Minister specifically allows otherwise — CCoP compliance is required while appealing", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 17", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The Minister may establish an Appeals Advisory Panel for appeals involving technical expertise", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 17", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The Minister's decision is final and may confirm, vary, or reverse the designation", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 17", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["The organization can suspend CCoP compliance while appealing the designation", "CCoP obligations do not apply until the appeal is resolved"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific court or tribunal handles CII designation appeals", "Inventing a specific fee or bond requirement for appeals"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Cybersecurity Act Part 3", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "designation_appeal_process", "related_sections": ["Section 11"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-006", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "We operate a telecommunications network in Singapore. The 2024 Cybersecurity Amendment Act introduced new categories: Essential Service CII (ESCI), Significant Telecommunications Infrastructure (STCC), and Foundational Digital Infrastructure (FDI). Our network may be designated as an STCC. What compliance obligations apply to STCCs under the amended regime?", "scenario_sector": "telecoms", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "The audited corpus used for this GT does not contain the 2024 amendment materials needed to give a source-backed answer on ESCI, STCC or FDI obligations. A grounded answer for this dataset is therefore that CCoP 2.0 governs designated CII under the current Cybersecurity Act / CCoP corpus, while any STCC-specific obligations would need to be answered from the amendment Act and any related CSA materials that are not part of this corpus.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "The 2024 Cybersecurity Amendment Act introduced new entity classifications including ESCI, STCC, and FDI beyond the original CII framework", "source": "Cybersecurity (Amendment) Act 2024", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "STCCs face specific cybersecurity obligations determined by the Commissioner — not necessarily the full CCoP 2.0 CII requirements", "source": "Cybersecurity (Amendment) Act 2024, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Designated organizations should engage directly with CSA sector officers to understand designation-specific compliance requirements", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "ESCI, STCC, and FDI designations each carry potentially different compliance obligations — organizations should not assume CCoP 2.0 automatically applies", "source": "Cybersecurity (Amendment) Act 2024", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["STCC designation automatically means full CCoP 2.0 applies", "The 2024 amendment replaced CII designation entirely"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming specific STCC compliance clauses that mirror CCoP 2.0 identically", "Inventing penalty amounts for STCC non-compliance"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Outside audited corpus - 2024 amendment regime", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "new_entity_classification", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Scope"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-007", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our maritime port authority has contracted out operation of our vessel traffic management system (VTMS) to a third-party managed services provider. The VTMS was designated as CII with us as the CIIO. Does our compliance obligation end now that day-to-day operations are handled by the third party?", "scenario_sector": "maritime", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "Your compliance obligation does not end with outsourcing. Under the Cybersecurity Act, the CIIO — the designated owner of the CII system — retains ultimate responsibility and accountability for CCoP 2.0 compliance regardless of operational outsourcing arrangements. You as the CIIO bear regulatory accountability even when day-to-day operations are performed by the managed services provider. CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements mandate that you maintain oversight of your service provider's cybersecurity practices affecting the CII. Critically, CCoP 2.0 requires that all vendor access to CII systems be performed on-site (not remote) unless explicitly approved. Your managed services agreement must specify security requirements aligned with CCoP 2.0 obligations. In a cybersecurity incident, CSA will hold the CIIO accountable — outsourcing does not transfer the 2-hour incident notification obligation or other CCoP duties.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "The CIIO retains ultimate CCoP 2.0 accountability regardless of operational outsourcing — the designated owner cannot contract away regulatory obligations", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11, CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requires all vendor access to CII systems to be performed on-site unless explicitly approved", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The 2-hour incident notification obligation to CSA remains with the CIIO, not the managed services provider", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 14", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Managed services agreements must specify CCoP-aligned security requirements to maintain compliance", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain clauses, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Outsourcing operations transfers CCoP compliance responsibility to the managed services provider", "The managed services provider becomes the accountable party for CII compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CSA will pursue the MSP rather than the CIIO in enforcement", "Inventing a clause exempting outsourced CII from owner responsibility"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Scope, Supply Chain", "clause_reference": ["14", "5.1.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "outsourced_cii_accountability", "related_sections": ["CCoP Section 5.5"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["Section 11 Cybersecurity Act", "Section 14 Cybersecurity Act", "CCoP 2.0 supply chain clauses"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "REPLACE"}}
{"test_id": "B01-008", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our aviation company operates the Changi Airport departure control system (IT) and the runway lighting control system (OT). Only the runway lighting system has been formally designated as CII. Does CCoP 2.0 Annex A apply to our departure control system?", "scenario_sector": "aviation", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "not-applicable", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 Annex A contains capabilities that are not included in the cybersecurity audit scope — they are recommended but not mandatory. For systems outside the CII digital boundary (such as the departure control system, which has not been designated), neither the main CCoP requirements nor Annex A carry mandatory force. CCoP 2.0's main body applies only to systems within the designated CII digital boundary. Annex A is non-auditable even for systems within the boundary. For the departure control system, no part of CCoP 2.0 is mandatorily applicable unless: (1) it is separately designated as CII, or (2) it falls within the digital boundary of the runway lighting CII as determined with CSA and the aviation sector lead. CSA recommends extending CCoP capabilities organization-wide, but this is guidance, not a mandate. Your Risk Manager should assess whether the departure control system's connection to CII creates boundary implications.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Annex A is non-auditable — it contains recommended capabilities not included in mandatory compliance scope", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Annex A, RESPONSE-TO-FEEDBACK Q2.2-2.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 applies only to systems within the designated CII digital boundary — non-designated systems are outside mandatory scope", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 2, Cybersecurity Act Section 11", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Systems outside the digital boundary are not subject to mandatory CCoP 2.0 requirements even if they interact with CII systems", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope section", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "CSA recommends extending CCoP capabilities organization-wide but this is guidance not a compliance obligation", "source": "RESPONSE-TO-FEEDBACK Q2.2-2.3", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Annex A is mandatory for all systems owned by a CIIO", "CCoP 2.0 Annex A applies to all systems interacting with CII"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming Annex A creates binding requirements on non-CII systems", "Inventing a specific Annex A clause number"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Annex A", "clause_reference": ["2.2"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "annex_a_applicability", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Scope"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-009", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our hospital IT systems were designated as CII in 2020 under CCoP 1.0. CCoP 2.0 introduced an OT Addendum in 2022. We have medical device networks (infusion pumps, imaging systems) that were not separately designated. Does the OT Addendum now apply to these medical devices?", "scenario_sector": "healthcare", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "partial", "expected_response": "The OT Addendum applicability depends on whether your medical device networks fall within the digital boundary of your designated CII. If the original designation was for hospital IT systems and the digital boundary was defined around those systems, the medical device networks are outside mandatory CCoP scope unless the boundary was expanded. However, if your medical device networks connect to or are interdependent with the designated IT systems, CSA and your Sector Lead may have included them within the digital boundary during CCoP 2.0 transition. You should review your designation documentation and engage CSA to confirm the current digital boundary definition. The OT Addendum applies to OT systems within the CII digital boundary — its introduction does not automatically extend to all OT-adjacent systems your organization operates. If the medical devices are within the boundary, the OT Addendum obligations apply from the CCoP 2.0 effective date.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "The OT Addendum applies to OT systems within the designated CII digital boundary — its introduction does not automatically extend to all organizational OT systems", "source": "CCoP 2.0 OT Addendum scope", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The digital boundary is jointly determined by CSA, the CIIO, and Sector Lead — the CIIO must confirm whether medical devices were included", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope section", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "New CCoP 2.0 requirements including the OT Addendum apply to systems within the digital boundary from the CCoP 2.0 effective date", "source": "CCoP 2.0 effective date guidance", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Systems with operational interdependencies with designated CII may be included in the digital boundary during boundary determination", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["The OT Addendum automatically applies to all medical devices once any CII is designated", "CCoP 1.0 CII designation scope automatically carries over to CCoP 2.0 without boundary review"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific OT Addendum clause number mandates medical device coverage", "Inventing exemptions for legacy medical devices"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 OT Addendum", "clause_reference": ["1.4.1", "1.2.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "ot_addendum_scope", "related_sections": ["Section 10 OT Security"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["CCoP 2.0 OT Addendum", "CCoP 2.0 Scope section"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "REJECT"}}
{"test_id": "B01-010", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "A small water utility with fewer than 50 employees received a CII designation notice last year. We do not have a dedicated cybersecurity team and have been trying to comply with CCoP 2.0. We cannot afford to implement all 220 clauses simultaneously. Is CCoP 2.0 applicability scaled to organizational size?", "scenario_sector": "water", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 applicability is not formally scaled to organizational size — all designated CIIOs must comply with CCoP 2.0 requirements regardless of how many staff they have. The standard does not contain a small organization exemption. However, some clauses reference implementing controls 'appropriate to the scale and complexity of the organization,' which provides implementation flexibility. For requirements your organization genuinely cannot meet due to resource constraints, the correct path is to submit waiver requests under Section 11(7) of the Cybersecurity Act for specific clauses, with documented compensating controls and a remediation timeline. CSA works with CIIOs including smaller organizations to develop practical compliance pathways. Prioritize clauses by criticality and risk — governance, access control, and incident response are foundational. Consider shared services arrangements or managed security service providers (MSSPs) to address capability gaps. The regulatory obligation is full compliance, but the implementation path can be phased with CSA engagement.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 compliance is mandatory for all designated CIIOs regardless of organizational size — there is no formal small organization exemption", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11, CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "For requirements that cannot be met, CIIOs should submit waiver requests under Section 11(7) with compensating controls and remediation timeline", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7)", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Some CCoP 2.0 clauses reference controls 'appropriate to the scale and complexity of the organization,' providing implementation flexibility", "source": "CCoP 2.0 governance clauses", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "CSA engages with CIIOs including smaller organizations to develop practical compliance pathways and phased implementation plans", "source": "Regulatory interpretation, CSA engagement practice", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Small organizations with fewer than 50 employees are exempt from specific CCoP clauses", "CCoP 2.0 compliance is optional for resource-constrained CIIOs"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific size threshold below which CCoP does not apply", "Inventing a CSA small CIIO programme with reduced requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Applicability", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "small_organization_applicability", "related_sections": ["CCoP Section 3 Governance"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-011", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our Singapore-based energy company operates a regional SCADA system that also monitors power infrastructure in Malaysia and Indonesia. The SCADA servers are physically located in Singapore. Does CCoP 2.0 apply to the entire SCADA system including the Malaysia and Indonesia monitoring functions?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "partial", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 applies to your CII system within Singapore's jurisdiction. Section 7 of the Cybersecurity Act requires that a computer system be located 'wholly or partly in Singapore' for designation — your SCADA servers are in Singapore, satisfying this criterion. The designation applies to the system as designated, which includes the Singapore-based SCADA infrastructure. If the Malaysia and Indonesia monitoring functions run on the same servers as the designated CII, they fall within the CII scope as part of the same system. If they operate as separate logical systems, the digital boundary determination (jointly with CSA) will clarify scope. The cross-border nature may create additional considerations for incident reporting and data sovereignty. CSA has jurisdiction over the Singapore-located components. Your organization should seek CSA clarification on whether the cross-border monitoring functions are considered part of the designated CII or a separate system outside scope.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Cybersecurity Act Section 7 requires systems to be located 'wholly or partly in Singapore' — Singapore-based SCADA servers satisfy this criterion", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 7(1)(b)", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 applies to the system as designated — the digital boundary determines whether cross-border monitoring functions are included", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope, Cybersecurity Act Section 7", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Cross-border components on the same servers as designated CII are within scope; separate logical systems require boundary determination", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "CIIOs with cross-border operations should seek CSA clarification on scope boundaries", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 applies to all systems globally once any Singapore system is designated", "Cross-border SCADA systems are automatically outside CCoP scope"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific clause defines cross-border scope limitations", "Inventing bilateral treaties between Singapore and Malaysia/Indonesia on CCoP recognition"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Scope", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "cross_border_scope", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 digital boundary definition"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-012", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "We provide banking payment processing services and are a subsidiary of a US parent company. Our Singapore core banking system is designated as CII. Our parent company's US security team manages our security operations remotely. Does CCoP 2.0 apply to the US parent company's security operations that affect our Singapore CII?", "scenario_sector": "banking", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 compliance obligations rest with the Singapore subsidiary as the designated CIIO, but those obligations extend to how the CII is managed regardless of where management occurs. The US parent company's security operations — to the extent they affect the Singapore CII — must comply with CCoP 2.0 requirements. Specifically, CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements mandate oversight of vendor and third-party access, including remote access. CCoP 2.0 requires that vendor access to CII be performed on-site in Singapore unless explicitly approved. Remote security operations from the US parent would likely require waiver or specific CSA approval. The Singapore CIIO cannot delegate away its compliance accountability to a parent company. If the US team has privileged access to the Singapore CII, CCoP access control, MFA, monitoring, and logging requirements must be satisfied for that access.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 obligations rest with the Singapore subsidiary as the designated CIIO — accountability cannot be delegated to a parent company", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requires vendor and third-party access to CII to be performed on-site in Singapore unless explicitly approved", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Remote access by US parent security operations must satisfy CCoP 2.0 access control, MFA, and monitoring requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5 Protection", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The Singapore CIIO must maintain oversight and accountability for security operations affecting the CII regardless of who performs them", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Parent company security operations are outside CCoP 2.0 scope if performed from outside Singapore", "The US parent company bears CCoP 2.0 accountability instead of the Singapore subsidiary"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming remote access from a parent company is automatically exempt", "Inventing an MNC exemption clause in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Scope, Supply Chain", "clause_reference": ["5.1.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "multinational_subsidiary_scope", "related_sections": ["CCoP Section 5.5 supply chain"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-013", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our transport company operates both a rail signalling system (OT, designated as CII) and a passenger information system (IT, not designated). The passenger information system feeds data into the signalling system's operator displays. Is the passenger information system within CCoP 2.0 scope?", "scenario_sector": "transport", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "partial", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 applies to the designated CII and the cyber operating environment defined for that CII. The passenger information system is not automatically in scope just because it exchanges data with the signalling system, but the interface and any dependency that can affect the signalling CII must be assessed and secured. The CIIO should determine whether the passenger information system forms part of the signalling system's cyber operating environment or creates a risk path into the designated CII and, if the designation or scope documents are unclear, confirm the scope with CSA. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.2.1, §1.4.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §7.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Systems with operational data interfaces to CII may be included in the digital boundary depending on the interface's security implications", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The digital boundary is jointly determined by CSA, the CIIO, and the Sector Lead — scope is not automatically defined by the organizational chart", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope section", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Where a non-designated system feeds data to CII operations, the interface itself may be within CCoP scope even if the source system is not", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "CIIOs should review designation documentation and seek CSA clarification on systems with operational data pathways to CII", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Non-designated systems connected to CII are automatically outside CCoP scope", "CCoP 2.0 never includes IT systems that interact with designated OT CII"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific clause automatically includes all systems with data interfaces", "Inventing a specific rule about passenger system classification"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Scope", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "1.4.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "interface_dependency_scope", "related_sections": ["Section 10 OT Addendum"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-014", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "We received a CII designation 3 years ago and have been complying with CCoP 2.0. CSA has now notified us that the digital boundary of our CII is being reviewed and may be expanded to include additional systems. What is our obligation during the review process?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "During a digital boundary review, your existing CCoP 2.0 obligations for the current designated systems remain fully in effect — the review does not create a compliance gap or pause. You are required to cooperate with CSA's review process, which may include providing documentation about the systems under consideration, their architecture, and their interconnections with the designated CII. If the boundary is expanded, the new systems will become subject to CCoP 2.0 requirements from the date the expanded boundary takes effect. You should begin gap assessment for the systems under review now, so that if they are included, you can accelerate compliance. Under the Cybersecurity Act, CIIOs have a duty to cooperate with CSA inspections and information requests. Failure to cooperate or provide accurate information during a boundary review could constitute a compliance breach.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Existing CCoP 2.0 obligations for currently designated systems remain fully in effect during a boundary review", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CIIOs must cooperate with CSA boundary reviews including providing documentation and access to information", "source": "Cybersecurity Act inspection powers", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "If the boundary is expanded, newly included systems become subject to CCoP 2.0 from the effective date of the expanded designation", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Beginning gap assessment for systems under boundary review review allows the CIIO to prepare for potential scope expansion", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["A boundary review suspends CCoP obligations for the systems under review", "Organizations have no obligation to cooperate with boundary reviews"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific review timeline is guaranteed by regulation", "Inventing appeal rights specific to boundary expansions distinct from designation appeals"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Scope", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "boundary_review_obligations", "related_sections": ["Section 17 appeals"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-015", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "I work at a media broadcasting company. Only our broadcast playout system is designated as CII. Our news production system — which creates content for broadcast — is on the same physical network. CCoP 2.0 requires network segmentation. Does this mean we need to segment the news production system from the broadcast CII?", "scenario_sector": "media", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "Yes, the connection should be segmented or otherwise tightly controlled to protect the broadcast CII. The key point is not that the news production system automatically becomes in-scope CII, but that §5.5 and §5.1.2 require the CIIO to secure network connectivity to and from the CII and to control access between parts of the environment commensurate with risk. If the news production system shares a network path with the broadcast CII, the CIIO should separate the networks or enforce controlled access points that restrict and monitor traffic into the broadcast environment. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §5.5, §5.1.2.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 network segmentation requirements apply to the CII system boundary — controlling all access to and from the CII regardless of the source system's designation status", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.4 network security", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Systems outside the CII boundary that share network access with CII must be segmented to protect the CII's network perimeter", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.4, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Controlled access points (firewall, access controls, logging) between news production and broadcast CII networks satisfy segmentation requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.4 network segmentation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Network segmentation is required to protect the CII perimeter — not because the news production system is itself in CCoP scope", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Systems outside the digital boundary are never subject to CCoP network requirements", "Network segmentation is only required between IT and OT systems"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific clause exempts media sector from network segmentation", "Inventing a specific approved topology for media broadcast CII"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.5", "5.1.2", "5.1.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "network_segmentation_scope", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Scope"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-016", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our security agency's command and dispatch system is designated as CII. CCoP 2.0 requires biennial cybersecurity audits. Our work involves classified information and national security. Are we allowed to use an internal audit team, or must we use an external auditor?", "scenario_sector": "security", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 requires cybersecurity audits to be conducted by qualified external auditors — internal teams alone do not satisfy the biennial audit requirement. The standard requires independence in audit conduct. However, for organizations handling classified information, CSA works with the relevant oversight body (in this case, the Ministry of Home Affairs or relevant national security authority) to ensure audit processes accommodate security classification requirements. Qualified external auditors working on classified CII systems must be appropriately cleared and operate under specific security protocols. The requirement for an external auditor does not disappear due to classification sensitivity — rather, the mechanism for selecting and using that auditor is adapted to national security constraints. Your organization should engage CSA and your sector lead to confirm the approved audit pathway for classified CII systems.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requires cybersecurity audits to be conducted by qualified external auditors — internal teams alone do not satisfy the biennial requirement", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 2.1.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "For classified CII systems, the external audit process is adapted to national security requirements in consultation with CSA and the relevant oversight body", "source": "Regulatory interpretation, CSA engagement practice", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "External auditors for classified CII must hold appropriate security clearances and operate under security protocols", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The classification of information does not exempt a CIIO from the external audit requirement — it affects how the audit is conducted", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Security agency CIIOs can use internal audit teams as a permanent substitute for external audits", "National security classification exempts organizations from CCoP 2.0 audit requirements"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific security agency is exempt from CCoP audits by ministerial order", "Inventing a separate CCoP version for security sector organizations"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 2: Audit Requirements", "clause_reference": ["9.2.4", "2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "audit_independence_classified", "related_sections": ["CCoP Section 3 Governance"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-017", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "What changed in CCoP 2.0 compared to CCoP 1.0 regarding access control requirements? Our organization complied with CCoP 1.0 and we want to identify the key gaps we need to close.", "scenario_sector": "banking", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 significantly expanded access control requirements compared to CCoP 1.0. Key changes include: (1) Multi-factor authentication (MFA) requirements were strengthened — CCoP 2.0 requires MFA for privileged access and remote access to CII, which was not as explicitly mandated in CCoP 1.0; (2) Privileged access management requirements were formalized — CCoP 2.0 introduced specific requirements for managing privileged accounts, session recording, and just-in-time access principles; (3) The OT Addendum introduced new access control requirements specifically for OT environments, including authentication methods appropriate for operational technology constraints; (4) Third-party and vendor access controls were tightened — CCoP 2.0 requires on-site vendor access and formal vendor access management. Organizations transitioning from CCoP 1.0 compliance should prioritize MFA implementation and privileged access management as the primary gaps, followed by OT-specific access controls if applicable.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 explicitly mandates MFA for privileged and remote access to CII — this was a significant strengthening from CCoP 1.0", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.1.5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 formalized privileged access management requirements including session recording and just-in-time access", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.3 privileged access management", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The OT Addendum introduced OT-specific access control requirements not present in CCoP 1.0", "source": "CCoP 2.0 OT Addendum", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Vendor and third-party access requirements were tightened in CCoP 2.0, requiring on-site access and formal vendor access management", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 access control requirements are identical to CCoP 1.0", "MFA was already mandated in full by CCoP 1.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Citing specific CCoP 1.0 clause numbers that do not exist", "Inventing specific dates when access control changes took effect"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.1.2", "5.3.1", "5.7.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "ccop_version_gap", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 OT Addendum"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-018", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "A foreign-owned telecommunications company has operations and network infrastructure in Singapore that has been designated as CII. The parent company is incorporated in Japan. Does CCoP 2.0 apply to this organization?", "scenario_sector": "telecoms", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "Yes, CCoP 2.0 applies to this organization. The Cybersecurity Act does not restrict CII designation to Singapore-incorporated companies — it applies to the owner of the computer system located in Singapore that meets the designation criteria. Foreign-owned companies operating in Singapore with designated CII systems are fully subject to CCoP 2.0 obligations. The Singapore subsidiary or Singapore-registered entity that owns the designated CII is the CIIO and bears all compliance obligations. The Japanese parent company's ownership does not create any exemption or reduction in requirements. In practice, the Singapore entity must demonstrate compliance, maintain the required documentation, and cooperate with CSA inspections and audits. Where the parent company's security policies or standards differ from CCoP 2.0, the Singapore CII must comply with CCoP 2.0 as the lex specialis regulatory requirement.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 applies to the owner of designated CII in Singapore regardless of the parent company's country of incorporation", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 7(1)(b), Section 11", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Foreign-owned companies are not exempt from CCoP 2.0 — the Singapore entity owning the designated CII is the CIIO with full obligations", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Where parent company security standards differ from CCoP 2.0, the Singapore CII must comply with CCoP 2.0 requirements", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The Singapore entity must maintain compliance documentation and cooperate with CSA regardless of parent company jurisdiction", "source": "Cybersecurity Act enforcement provisions", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Foreign-owned companies are exempt from CCoP 2.0 if headquartered outside Singapore", "Japan-Singapore bilateral arrangements reduce CCoP compliance obligations"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific exemption for foreign MNCs in CCoP 2.0", "Inventing a bilateral agreement between Singapore and Japan on cybersecurity standards"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Applicability", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "foreign_ownership_applicability", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Scope"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-019", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our water utility's treatment plant SCADA is designated as CII. We use a shared services arrangement where a third-party IT services company manages our IT systems including the corporate LAN. The corporate LAN connects to the SCADA network via a DMZ. Is the IT services company required to comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "water", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "partial", "expected_response": "The IT services company is not itself designated as a CIIO and does not bear primary CCoP 2.0 obligations. However, to the extent they manage systems within the digital boundary or have access to CII systems, CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements impose obligations on you as the CIIO to ensure your vendor meets required security standards. CCoP 2.0 requires that vendor access to CII be performed on-site and that vendors with access to CII systems are subject to appropriate security requirements enforced through contractual obligations. The shared services provider managing the corporate LAN that connects to the SCADA DMZ represents a supply chain risk that CCoP 2.0 expects you to manage. You must assess whether the corporate LAN's management falls within the digital boundary. If it does, the IT services company's activities on that network must comply with CCoP requirements. If outside the boundary, vendor risk management controls still apply.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Third-party service providers are not themselves designated CIIOs but must meet security requirements specified by the CIIO in contracts", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requires vendor access to CII to be performed on-site and subject to appropriate security controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain clauses", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The CIIO must assess whether systems managed by the shared services provider fall within the digital boundary of the CII", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Vendor risk management requirements apply even to service providers managing systems outside the formal digital boundary if those systems have connectivity to CII", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain risk requirements", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Third-party managed systems are automatically outside CCoP scope", "IT service providers have no CCoP obligations regardless of CII access"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific clause exempts managed service arrangements", "Inventing a CSA whitelist of approved IT service providers"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Supply Chain", "clause_reference": ["5.1.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "shared_services_scope", "related_sections": ["CCoP Section 5.5"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-020", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "CCoP 2.0 was issued in July 2022. Our biennial cybersecurity audit was last completed in February 2022 under CCoP 1.0. When is our next audit due under CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "government", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "Your next audit is due by February 2024 — 24 months from your last audit completion in February 2022. CSA clarified that for recurring requirements like biennial audits, deadlines are based on when the previous instance was performed, even if it was conducted under CCoP 1.0. The transition to CCoP 2.0 does not reset the clock or extend the audit cycle. Your February 2024 audit must be conducted against CCoP 2.0 requirements, not CCoP 1.0. This means your organization needed to be assessed against the expanded ~220 clause standard from your first post-transition audit. If your February 2024 audit has already passed without completion, you are in breach of CCoP 2.0 audit requirements and should engage CSA immediately to remediate the situation.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Biennial audit deadlines are based on when the previous audit was performed — transitioning to CCoP 2.0 does not reset the 24-month clock", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 2.1.1, RESPONSE-TO-FEEDBACK Q6.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The next audit must be conducted against CCoP 2.0 requirements even if the previous audit was under CCoP 1.0", "source": "CCoP 2.0 effective date, Section 2.1.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Section 2.1.1 requires audits at least once every 2 years (24 months)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 2.1.1", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "CIIOs with overdue audits should engage CSA proactively to remediate the compliance gap", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["The CCoP 2.0 transition resets the 24-month audit cycle to start from July 2022", "CCoP 1.0 audit completion satisfies CCoP 2.0 biennial requirements indefinitely"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific grace period for first CCoP 2.0 audit post-transition", "Inventing a specific CSA directive extending audit deadlines"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 2: Audit Requirements", "clause_reference": ["1.5.1", "1.5.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "audit_cycle_transition", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Section 2"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-021", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our healthcare organisation's SingHealth cluster has two systems: a clinical management system handling patient records (designated as CII) and a research database containing anonymized patient data for clinical trials (not designated). A ransomware attack encrypts the research database. Does CCoP 2.0 incident reporting apply?", "scenario_sector": "healthcare", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "not-applicable", "expected_response": "A ransomware incident affecting only the non-designated research database does not automatically trigger the CII incident-reporting obligation just because the organisation also operates designated CII. The grounded question is whether the incident affects, or creates a risk of affecting, the designated clinical management CII. The CIIO should assess the connectivity and potential impact on the designated CII, activate the incident-response process as needed, and ensure that the incident-reporting structure in the IR plan complies with the Cybersecurity Act and other applicable laws. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §7.1.1(b); Cybersecurity Act 2018 §14.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "The 2-hour CSA incident notification requirement applies to cybersecurity incidents affecting designated CII systems — not all organizational systems", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 14", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "An incident affecting a non-designated system outside the digital boundary does not trigger CCoP 2.0 incident reporting unless it affects or risks the CII", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 14, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CIIOs should assess and notify CSA proactively if there is any risk of the incident spreading to or affecting the designated CII", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "PDPC personal data breach notification obligations may apply separately to the research database incident regardless of CII status", "source": "PDPC breach notification requirements", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All cybersecurity incidents at a CIIO trigger the 2-hour notification regardless of which system is affected", "Ransomware on a non-CII system at a healthcare CIIO automatically triggers CCoP 2.0 incident reporting"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific clause mandates reporting all incidents at a CIIO", "Inventing a threshold of data sensitivity that triggers CCoP notification"]}, "metadata": {"section": "7", "clause_reference": ["7.1.1(b)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "incident_reporting_scope", "related_sections": ["PDPC breach notification"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["section 14"]}}
{"test_id": "B01-022", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "We are a media company and CSA has notified us that our broadcast infrastructure may be considered for CII designation. We understand the energy and banking sectors are clearly CII — but is media broadcasting considered a critical sector, and what is the threshold for designation?", "scenario_sector": "media", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "applicable", "expected_response": "Yes, media is one of Singapore's 11 designated critical sectors under the Cybersecurity Act. The 11 sectors are: energy, water, banking and finance, healthcare, aviation, land transport, maritime, info-communications, government, media, and security and emergency services. Being in a critical sector does not automatically mean your systems will be designated — the Commissioner designates specific computer systems based on the criteria in Section 7(1): the system must be necessary for the continuous delivery of an essential service, and its loss or compromise must have a debilitating effect on essential service availability in Singapore, and be located in Singapore. For media, this typically means broadcast infrastructure critical to national emergency communications or critical broadcast services. Your organization should engage CSA's Media sector officer to understand the specific designation criteria being applied to your systems.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Media is one of Singapore's 11 designated critical sectors under the Cybersecurity Act", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Schedule 1 (critical sectors)", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CII designation criteria: system must be necessary for essential service delivery AND its loss must have debilitating effect on availability in Singapore", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 7(1)", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Being in a critical sector does not automatically result in designation — specific systems meeting Section 7(1) criteria are designated", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 7", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The list of specifically designated CII systems is classified for national security reasons — only the 11 critical sectors are publicly disclosed", "source": "Singapore Cybersecurity Act public documentation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All media companies in Singapore are designated as CIIOs", "Media is not considered a critical sector under Singapore's Cybersecurity Act"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming the number of designated CII systems is publicly known", "Inventing specific revenue or audience thresholds for media CII designation"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Cybersecurity Act Part 3", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "sector_classification", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Scope"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-023", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "Our bank's CII designation covers our core banking system. We recently acquired a fintech company that processes card payments using a separate technical platform. The acquired company's systems are not designated as CII. Does acquiring a non-CII entity bring those systems into CCoP 2.0 scope?", "scenario_sector": "banking", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "not-applicable", "expected_response": "Acquiring a company does not automatically bring that company's systems into CCoP 2.0 scope. CCoP 2.0 applies to designated CII systems as specified in the designation notice — acquisition of a company with undesignated systems does not extend the existing designation. However, several post-acquisition considerations apply: (1) You must notify CSA of material changes to the organization and assess whether the acquired systems create new connections or dependencies with the designated CII; (2) If the acquired card payment systems are connected to your designated core banking CII, this may affect the digital boundary and should be reviewed with CSA; (3) The Commissioner may subsequently designate the acquired systems as CII if they meet Section 7 criteria; (4) Your existing CII designation does not automatically expand to cover the acquired systems. The acquired systems must be assessed for CII designation risk, and any connections to the existing CII digital boundary must be carefully managed.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Corporate acquisition does not automatically expand an existing CII designation to cover acquired systems", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 7, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Material changes to the CIIO organization should be notified to CSA, including acquisitions that create new connections to the designated CII", "source": "Regulatory interpretation, CIIO notification obligations", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The Commissioner may subsequently designate acquired systems if they meet Section 7(1) criteria after review", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 7", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Connections between acquired systems and existing CII may affect the digital boundary and require CSA review", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope section", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All systems owned by a CIIO become CII upon acquisition", "A corporate acquisition automatically expands the CII designation"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific deadline by which acquired systems must be brought into CCoP scope", "Inventing a formal merger notification obligation with CSA within specific days"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Scope", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "acquisition_scope", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Scope"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-024", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "CCoP 2.0 mentions Annex A as non-auditable capabilities. Our CSA sector officer suggested we consider Annex A controls for our energy CII. Is there any circumstance where Annex A becomes mandatory?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "not-applicable", "expected_response": "Annex A contains capabilities that are explicitly excluded from the mandatory cybersecurity audit scope — they are aspirational best practices that CSA encourages but cannot audit or enforce as mandatory requirements under CCoP 2.0. There is no provision in CCoP 2.0 that converts Annex A controls into mandatory requirements. However, two practical scenarios can make Annex A effectively important: (1) If a specific Annex A control becomes a compensating control in a waiver application, implementing it demonstrates security commitment even if not formally mandatory; (2) A sector-specific addendum or future CCoP revision could incorporate Annex A controls into the main body, making them auditable. Your CSA sector officer's suggestion likely reflects a recommendation for security maturity, not a compliance mandate. Annex A implementation enhances security posture and may support waiver applications, but non-implementation cannot result in a compliance finding.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Annex A controls are explicitly excluded from the mandatory cybersecurity audit scope — they are non-auditable", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Annex A, RESPONSE-TO-FEEDBACK Q2.2-2.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "No provision in CCoP 2.0 converts Annex A controls into mandatory requirements — non-implementation cannot result in a compliance finding", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Annex A", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Annex A controls can be used as compensating controls in waiver applications even though not mandatory", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Future CCoP revisions could incorporate Annex A controls into the mandatory main body — this is a risk to monitor", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Annex A controls are mandatory for high-risk sectors like energy", "CSA sector officers can mandate Annex A implementation for individual CIIOs"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CSA issued a directive making specific Annex A controls mandatory for energy", "Inventing a sector-specific Annex A enforcement mechanism"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Annex A", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "annex_a_mandatory_scope", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Scope"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B01-025", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B01", "input": {"question": "We are an aviation company and our Air Traffic Control (ATC) systems are designated as CII. A system failure occurs in our ATC that we believe was caused by a software bug, not a cyberattack. Do we still need to notify CSA within 2 hours?", "scenario_sector": "aviation", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "partial", "expected_response": "This depends on whether the incident constitutes a 'cybersecurity incident' under the Cybersecurity Act. The Act defines a cybersecurity incident as an act or activity carried out without lawful authority that jeopardises or threatens to jeopardise the security, integrity, confidentiality, or availability of a computer or computer system. A software bug causing a system failure may not meet this definition if it is a purely technical fault without any malicious or unauthorized access component. However, you should not wait for certainty before assessing notification obligations. In practice, significant system failures at CII should be investigated immediately for signs of unauthorized activity — what appears to be a software bug may reveal an underlying cyber cause. The safe approach is to: (1) Immediately investigate for cybersecurity indicators; (2) If any cyber cause cannot be excluded within the 2-hour window, notify CSA; (3) Update the notification as the investigation progresses. CSA would prefer an early notification that is subsequently downgraded over a missed mandatory notification.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Section 14 notification obligations apply to 'cybersecurity incidents' as defined by the Act — purely technical failures without unauthorized access may not qualify", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 14, Section 2 definitions", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CIIOs should immediately investigate system failures for cybersecurity indicators and notify CSA if any cyber cause cannot be excluded within 2 hours", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 14, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The definition of cybersecurity incident requires an act carried out without lawful authority that jeopardises system security, integrity, confidentiality, or availability", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 2 definitions", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "CSA guidance supports early precautionary notification followed by updates as investigation progresses rather than delaying notification pending certainty", "source": "Regulatory interpretation, CSA engagement practice", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Software bugs never trigger CCoP incident notification requirements", "All system failures at CII automatically require 2-hour CSA notification regardless of cause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific threshold of downtime that triggers mandatory notification", "Inventing separate notification procedures for technical vs cyber incidents"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 8: Response and Recovery", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "incident_definition_ambiguity", "related_sections": ["CCoP Section 7 Response and Recovery"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
